#Stripe:

💳 Stripe Payment Flow (Step-by-Step in Simple English)
🧩 1️⃣ Backend Creates a PaymentIntent

What happens:

Your backend (e.g., Spring Boot) calls Stripe’s API using your secret key.

It tells Stripe:

“Hey Stripe, I want to collect ₹1000 from the customer for this order.”

Stripe replies:

{
  "id": "pi_12345",
  "client_secret": "pi_12345_secret_abcxyz"
}


Meaning:
Backend just informs Stripe how much money is needed — it doesn’t touch or store card details.

💳 2️⃣ Frontend Gets the client_secret

What happens:

Your frontend (React app) receives the client_secret from backend.

It uses the Stripe library (@stripe/react-stripe-js or similar) to confirm payment:

stripe.confirmCardPayment(clientSecret, {
  payment_method: {
    card: cardElement, // user's card input
    billing_details: { name: "John Doe" }
  }
});


Meaning:
Frontend tells Stripe:

“Here’s the customer’s card info for PaymentIntent pi_12345 — please charge it.”

🔐 3️⃣ Stripe Checks Everything

What happens:

Stripe verifies that the client_secret is valid and matches a real PaymentIntent created by your backend.

Stripe safely processes the card payment.

Meaning:
Stripe double-checks that this request belongs to your Stripe account and not someone else’s fake request.

💬 4️⃣ Stripe Responds

What happens:

Stripe tells your frontend:

✅ Success: Payment completed successfully.

❌ Failed: Card declined or insufficient funds.

Meaning:
Frontend just displays success/failure — Stripe handles all real payment logic securely.

| Step                 | Who Does It        | What It Means                                      |
| -------------------- | ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------- |
| Create PaymentIntent | Backend            | “Stripe, please set up a payment of ₹1000.”        |
| Return client_secret | Backend → Frontend | “Here’s the token for this payment.”               |
| Confirm payment      | Frontend           | “Stripe, charge this card for that ₹1000 payment.” |
| Process payment      | Stripe             | “Okay, payment done.”                              |

⚠️ Important Rules
❌ Never send raw card info to your backend.
✅ Always create the PaymentIntent on your backend.
✅ Frontend only uses the client_secret to confirm payment.
💳 Stripe handles all sensitive card data securely.

🪄 Analogy (Real-World Example)
Imagine you’re buying a phone online:
Backend → Tells the store: “This person wants to buy a phone for ₹1000.”
Stripe (the store system) → Gives you an order slip (client_secret).
Frontend (you) → Go to the counter (Stripe site) and give your card.
Stripe → Verifies the slip and charges your card.
Frontend → Gets “Payment successful!” and shows it.

#StripeServiceImpl.java
Purpose:
    Handles actual Stripe API calls.

Steps:
    Initialize Stripe API key (from application.properties).
    Search for existing customer by email.
    If found → reuse customer; else create a new one.
    Create payment intent linked to that customer.
    Return the PaymentIntent.
✅ Notes:
    Customer = Stripe’s record of your app’s user (email, name, address).
    This helps you view all that user’s payments from Stripe dashboard later.
    Linking payment intents to customers keeps history, refund, and subscription management easy.

